## Proof

We determine the VC-dimension of the class
$$H_{N\text{-parity}} = \{h_I : I \subseteq \{1,\dots,N\}\}, \qquad h_I(x) = \Big(\sum_{i\in I} x_i\Big)\bmod 2.$$

**Upper bound.** The class contains exactly $2^N$ functions (one for each subset $I \subseteq \{1,\dots,N\}$). To shatter a set of $m$ points requires realizing all $2^m$ possible binary labelings, so we need $2^m \le 2^N$, i.e. $m \le N$. Hence $\text{VCdim}(H_{N\text{-parity}}) \le N$.

**Lower bound (shattering $N$ points).** Consider the $N$ standard basis vectors $e_1, \dots, e_N \in \{0,1\}^N$, where $e_j$ has a $1$ in coordinate $j$ and $0$ elsewhere. For any desired labeling $(y_1,\dots,y_N) \in \{0,1\}^N$, set
$$I = \{j : y_j = 1\}.$$
Then
$$h_I(e_j) = \Big(\sum_{i\in I} (e_j)_i\Big)\bmod 2 = [j \in I] = y_j.$$
Thus every one of the $2^N$ labelings of $\{e_1,\dots,e_N\}$ is realized by some $h_I \in H_{N\text{-parity}}$, so these $N$ points are shattered. Hence $\text{VCdim}(H_{N\text{-parity}}) \ge N$.

Combining both bounds, $\text{VCdim}(H_{N\text{-parity}}) = N$.

